  <h2>Does the white background have to be pure white?</h2>

  <p>In words, every source that describes it says yes. As a number, the record is younger and thinner than it looks.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The most commonly published background for the white-background image is pure white. Eight of fifteen sources call for pure white, and every source that prints a number prints RGB 255 255 255. The number is recent: every source dated 2017 to 2024 describes pure white in words and prints no value, and all three that print the triplet are dated 2026.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources, Chinese-language first, collected 10 September 2026, counted by domain and grouped by publication date. Modal value with its agreement count, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>The harder question for a retoucher is how close to white is close enough, and there the sources split.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Thirteen of fifteen sources state no tolerance at all. One publishes a tolerance of plus or minus 3, counting 252 to 255 on each channel as passing, on a page whose own publisher gives 255 flat on its specification page. One says any near-white fails and describes a background at 254 254 255 as detectable. No source describes any value below 252 as acceptable.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources, Chinese-language, collected 10 September 2026, each published tolerance position counted separately. Contested modal position, reported as the spread rather than a single value, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>Put those two positions together and one choice survives both.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">RGB 255 255 255 is the only background value that no published reading rejects. A background at 252 passes the one published tolerance and fails the one published zero-tolerance reading. Clipping the background to pure 255 is therefore the one choice the published record does not contradict. That is a conclusion drawn from the spread, not a platform rule.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: derived from the published tolerance positions above, fifteen independent sources collected 10 September 2026, reasoning stated openly and resting on modal values rather than primary readings.</p>

  <p>Clip to 255. It costs a retoucher nothing, and it is the only value on the published record that nobody says fails.</p>

  <h2>Can a model appear in a white-background image?</h2>

  <p>No, on every source that addresses it, and the prohibitions are the most consistent part of the whole record.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The prohibitions are the most consistent part of the published record. Nine of fifteen sources rule out text, watermarks, logos and promotional overlay. Seven rule out a model, six rule out shadows, six rule out spliced composites, and five rule out hangers, mannequins and tags. One extends the model ban to any body part at all, hands, feet, legs or a head.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources, Chinese-language first, collected 10 September 2026, counted by domain for each prohibited element. Modal positions with their agreement counts, not a reading from the platform.</p>

