  <p>The shadow row carries a caveat worth reading before a retoucher removes one.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Whether a faint shadow directly under the product survives is not settled. Six of fifteen sources prohibit shadows with no exception. Two, both published in 2026, describe a light contact shadow as tolerated, and one of those two says the opposite on another of its own pages. Reflections and glare are named as prohibited on one source only.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources, Chinese-language, collected 10 September 2026, counted by domain with self-contradicting publishers noted. Contested modal position, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>Six sources against two, with one of the two contradicting itself. Remove the shadow.</p>

  <h2>How much of the frame should the product fill?</h2>

  <p>Here the published guidance has changed shape over time, and the two shapes point in different directions.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published fill guidance for the white-background image comes in two generations. Five pages dated 2017 to 2024 say the product should fill the frame and leave no white margin. Three pages dated 2026 give a number instead, 70 to 80 percent of the frame, which leaves 20 to 30 percent white. The full numeric spread runs from 60 to 80 percent, and no source states where either version came from.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources, Chinese-language, collected 10 September 2026, grouped by publication date and reported as the spread rather than averaged. Contested for the figure, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>No margin against a fifth of the frame left white is not a small disagreement. The newer numeric guidance is the more cautious working choice, but neither version has a stated origin, so this page publishes the band and not a cap.</p>

  <h2>A file weight that looks confirmed and is not</h2>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The 38KB to 300KB file weight that circulates for the white-background image traces to a single origin. It appears on three of fifteen sources. The oldest is dated September 2017, and the two later pages repeat that page's size, format and prohibition list almost line for line. Other sources give a 3MB ceiling instead. Three pages restating one page are one data point, not three.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources, Chinese-language, collected 10 September 2026, with each figure traced to its earliest dated appearance. Contested, originating once, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>A figure copied twice from a 2017 page looks like agreement in a count and is really one voice. Staying under 300KB satisfies both readings anyway.</p>

  <h2>Where does Tmall use the white-background image?</h2>

  <p>In recommendation, according to the sources. Not in search, according to none of them.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The white-background image is described as feeding recommendation and content channels on the Taobao app homepage. Eight of fifteen sources say so, with the channel names changing as the pages get newer. Six of fifteen say a listing without a compliant white image loses eligibility for those channels. No source in the collection says the image is used in search results, and none puts a number on the exposure lost.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources, Chinese and English, collected 10 September 2026, counted by domain. Modal position with its agreement count, the absence stated with its count, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>The slot moves by category, and on JD it moves to the front.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published category standards move the white-background image. A reproduction of the food standard puts it second. A 2017 tutorial puts it second for luggage, and apparel is published both as the fifth image (2017) and as the first (2026). On JD, every page naming a slot puts the white image first, against Tmall's fifth, five of five pages per platform with none contradicting.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources collected 10 September 2026, one source per category map, and a separate twenty-four-page JD and Tmall collection from the same date. Contested for each category map, consensus for the JD against Tmall slot, not a reading from either platform.</p>

  <h2>Why white-background images fail</h2>

  <p>No rejection notice was obtained for this page, and none is reconstructed. No source ranks the causes either, so what follows is an unranked list.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No published source ranks why white-background images fail. Three of fifteen describe causes, as an unranked list: a background that is not pure white, a shadow, a model, text or a logo, a product too small in the frame, a duplicated product, and rough cutout edges. None says how often any of them occurs.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: fifteen independent published sources, Chinese-language, collected 10 September 2026. Stated as an unranked list with its count, not as a platform rejection taxonomy.</p>

  <p>Each listed cause has a corresponding fix. The table below is an illustration of that checklist, built from the causes above, and not a record of any rejected listing.</p>

